m step by step. Give your final answer in \\boxed{...}.\n\nWhat is the smallest integer value of $c$ such that the function $f(x)=\\frac{x^2+1}{x^2-x+c}$ has a domain of all real numbers?","role":"user"}],"rendered_sha256":"80252fe70f25384d2a6ea722ac09d0cfbf88c2fcaaf41abe232f07333a0619da"},"seconds":9.721309185028076,"seed":916779086740844564,"stop":"eos","text":"To determine the smallest integer value of \\( c \\) such that the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - x + c} \\) has a domain of all real numbers, we need to ensure that the denominator \\( x^2 - x + c \\) is never zero for any real number \\( x \\). This means the quadratic equation \\( x^2 - x + c = 0 \\) should have no real roots.\n\nA quadratic equation \\( ax^2 + bx + c = 0 \\) has no real roots if its discriminant is less than zero. The discriminant \\(\\Delta\\) of the quadratic equation \\( x^2 - x + c = 0 \\) is given by:\n\\[\n\\Delta = b^2 - 4ac\n\\]\nHere, \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -1 \\), and \\( c = c \\). Substituting these values into the discriminant formula, we get:\n\\[\n\\Delta = (-1)^2 - 4 \\cdot 1 \\cdot c = 1 - 4c\n\\]\nFor the quadratic equation to have no real roots, the discriminant must be less than zero:\n\\[\n1 - 4c < 0\n\\]\nSolving this inequality for \\( c \\):\n\\[\n1 < 4c\n\\]\n\\[\n\\frac{1}{4} < c\n\\]\n\\[\nc > \\frac{1}{4}\n\\]\nThe smallest integer greater than \\(\\frac{1}{4}\\) is \\( 1 \\). Therefore, the smallest integer value of \\( c \\) such that the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{x^2 + 1}{x^2 - x + c} \\) has a domain of all real numbers is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{1}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{1}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"algebra:446"}
